ployed solely as a qualitative/literary anchor \u2014 never as a measured output of this design. The document distinguishes design (what will be run and how analyzed) from execution (nothing run yet).\n\n## 1. Research question (operationalized)\n*How well does QuaRot generalize to a non-LLAMA model (Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct), and how do QuaRot-RTN and QuaRot-GPTQ compare in perplexity and zero-shot accuracy at 4-, 6-, and 8-bit?*\n\nOperational definition: quantify per-condition **degradation against the FP16 baseline** (PPL-ratio and zero-shot accuracy drop) and compute **\u0394-metrics between methods per bit-width**. Answer the question in terms of (a) absolute quality per method\u00d7width, (b) method ordering (which is better, and where they converge), and (c) whether the rotation benefit transfers to a non-LLAMA architecturally (via the optional non-rotated control and the literary comparison with published Llama results).\n\n## 2. Fixed/independent variables\n- **Model (single):** `microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct` (3.8B, 4k context, instruct/chat variant). No multi-model sweep.\n- **Precisions:** INT4, INT6, INT8 \u2014 weight-only quantization (activations kept FP16; QuaRot rotations already handle activation outliers).\n- **Quantization pipelines:** **QuaRot-RTN** and **QuaRot-GPTQ**, with one **identical shared rotation configuration** applied to both methods (residual-stream rotation + attention QK/PV rotations, Hadamard, per source setup). The only difference between the two arms is the weight quantizer (RTN vs GPTQ).\n\n## 3. Controls\n- **Primary control (mandatory):** FP16 unquantized **same-checkpoint** model \u2014 no rotation, no quantization \u2014 evaluated identically on both metrics. This anchors \"generalization\" as degradation vs unquantized.\n- **Secondary control (optional, conditional on budget, NOT mandatory):** RTN and GPTQ **without rotations** at each bit-width, to isolate the contribution of QuaRot's rotations for a non-LLAMA arch. If computed, placed in an appendix; its absence does not invalidate the core matrix.\n\n## 4. Metrics protocol (confirmed)\n- **Perplexity:** WikiText-2 **test** split, **no chat template**, **no sliding window**, **sequence length 2048** tokens. Compute mean tokenwise negative log-likelihood \u2192 exp(PPL). Lower is better.\n- **Zero-shot accuracy:** EleutherAI **lm-eval** harness over the core set \u2014 **PIQA, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, HellaSwag, Winogrande** \u2014 run in **chat/multiturn mode** for the instruct variant (per Phi-3 model-card convention / source setup). Report **per-task accuracy + mean accuracy** across the 5 tasks. Exact-match accuracy.\n\n## 5. Experimental matrix and replication\n- **7 core conditions:** `FP16` \u222a `{RTN, GPTQ} \u00d7 {4,6,8}`.\n- **GPTQ calibration:** C4, **128 sequences \u00d7 2048 tokens**, group size 128, symmetric, fixed quantizer hyperparameters; repeated with **2\u20133 calibration seeds**.\n- **Per-seed recorded metrics (for GPTQ):** WikiText-2 PPL and each of the five zero-shot accuracies plus the 5-task mean. Thus every seed yields a full `{PPL, per-task acc, mean acc}` vector.\n- **Summary rules:**\n  - GPTQ cells report **mean \u00b1 range** across seeds for every metric (PPL and each accuracy).\n  - RTN and FP16 are deterministic \u2192 single values.\n- Resulting master table: rows = 7 conditions; columns = PPL, 5 per-task accuracies, mean accuracy (GPTQ rows show mean\u00b1range).\n\n## 6. Comparative analysis plan\n1. **Full results table** (Section 5) + condition-level PPL and mean-accuracy columns.\n2. **\u0394-metrics per bit-width (RTN vs GPTQ):**\n   - \u0394PPL = PPL_method(bit) \u2212 PPL_baseline (and PPL-ratio = PPL_quant/PPL_fp16);\n   - \u0394zero-shot = acc_baseline \u2212 acc_method(bit);\n   - state which method wins at each width, where they converge (expected: larger RTN/GPTQ gap at 4-bit, near-parity at 8-bit \u2014 stated as a hypothesis to test, not a result).\n3. **Calibration-sensitivity:** use the GPTQ mean\u00b1range to judge whether seed spread is material at each bit-width (narrow at 8-bit, wider at 4-bit expected).\n4. **Degradation indices (generalization indicators):** PPL-ratio and zero-shot accuracy drop vs FP16; lower ratio / smaller drop = better generalization of QuaRot to Phi-3 at that method\u00d7width.\n5. **External anchor (qualitative, literary only):** compare derived degradation ratios and method ordering with **published QuaRot results on Llama-2/3** (papers/leaderboards). Frame strictly as \"how Phi-3 behaves relative to published LLAMA trends\"; never presented as measured data in this design.\n6. **Optional appendix:** non-rotated control and chat-template sensitivity (single canonical template vs model-card default).\n\n## 7. Feasibility / design notes (not execution)\n- **Architectural mapping of QuaRot hooks to Phi-3 (design-time analysis):** verify config `model_type` handling (custom \"phi3\" type) and map QuaRot's expected Llama-like module names to Phi-3 names: `model.layers[]` blocks; `self_attn.q_proj/k_proj/v_proj/o_proj`; `mlp.gate_up_proj` (fused gate/up) and `mlp.down_proj`; RMSNorm locations; rotary/hidden-size details. Confirm placement of residual-stream rotation per decoder layer and QK rotation (before attention) / PV rotation (after attention), respecting the 4k context (block-wise rotation is context-agnostic, so 4k is fine). Produce an explicit layer-name mapping table and a `get_rotation`-style dispatch keyed on the Phi-3 config as a design prerequisite.\n- **Quantization-library support check:** verify chosen library (e.g., gptqmodel/auto-gptq with QuaRot rotation hooks) supports **group size 128 at INT4, INT6, INT8**; GPTQ typically covers 2\u20138 bit but the 6-bit+group-128 path must be confirmed for the specific library version. RTN is trivially supported at all three widths.\n- **Compute sizing:** Phi-3-mini \u2248 3.8B \u2192 FP16 ~7.7GB weights; INT4 << 2GB. Perplexity and 5-task zero-shot eval 